+\section*{Abstract}
+\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
+
+A digitally controlled USB-C power supply was implemented using an inherited charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant topology and planar magnetics. Burst control was developed for secondary-bulk regulation, startup sequencing, and energy limitation at no load and after load removal.
+
+The gallium-nitride (GaN) half bridge operated at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}. Average power was controlled by enabling bursts of switching cycles. A circular direct memory access (DMA) buffer supplied the feedback samples. Startup precharge and separate duty limits for loaded and no-load operation were implemented. The USB-C PD stage provided the tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings.
+
+At \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz} input, matched measurements at the \SI{20}{\volt} setting yielded \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt} recorded peak-to-peak ripple. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. Paired input/output readings gave preliminary efficiency estimates of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent}, limited by acquisition timing and calibration uncertainty. The tests included no-load operation and load removal. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum, motivating further loss and thermal characterization.
+
+V2 integrated the MCU, GaN stage, isolated feedback, and USB-C circuit on a four-layer PCB. Lower half-bridge heating was observed after tank and timing changes, while input-bridge heating remained unresolved (\cref{sec:res-v2-testing}). Bench performance remained comparable to the first prototype. Winding loss and the input current path remained possible limits on output power. The V3 edge-card layout retained secondary rectification and omitted the USB-PD output section, giving an estimated net volume reduction of approximately \SI{8}{\percent}. Replacement planar magnetics were not installed. The external regulated USB-PD stage must be included when comparing the volume of complete supplies.
+
+Digitally controlled operation near \SI{80}{\watt} was demonstrated. Operation at the \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and \SI{65}{\watt} minimum still needs long-duration thermal and stability tests. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC remain unverified.
